 much weaker NFE2L2 dependency, accepted as H13 estimate +0.796, after the initially registered stronger-dependency direction H8 was rejected as directionally opposite. TP53 loss is associated with weaker USP7 dependency, H9 validated. PTEN loss is associated with stronger STAT3 dependency, H42 validated. These effects are far larger than the threshold for a meaningful difference and their intervals exclude zero, even under stricter validation intervals for the validated claims.\n\nNegative, unsupported, or directionally opposite findings. Many common-driver probes were unsupported: KRAS mutation with KRAS dependency H1, BRAF V600E with BRAF dependency H2 and H41, EGFR amplification with EGFR dependency H3 and H16, ERBB2 amplification with ERBB2 dependency H4, PIK3CA mutation with EGFR dependency in adenocarcinoma H30, NF1 loss with KRAS dependency H44, STK11 loss with NFE2L2 dependency H37, MYC amplification with DHX9 H11 or STAT3 H12, and BRCA2 loss with DHX9 H28. TP53 loss showed no support for MDM2 H5 or RPL11 H10 differences. ARID1A loss was not associated with SMARCA2 dependency overall H40, and ARID1A did not modify the SMARCA4-loss effect H26. Heterogeneity tests for accepted effects were mostly null: subtype did not materially modify SMARCA4-SMARCA2 H38 or KEAP1-NFE2L2 H39; mutation burden, screen quality, and other tested modifiers did not support important modification of the SMARCA4-SMARCA2, WRN MSI-high, KEAP1-NFE2L2, or PTEN-STAT3 associations where tested, including screen-quality interaction for USP7 H33, mutation-burden interaction for SMARCA4 H34, TP53 co-loss for NFE2L2 H36, upper-quartile mutation-burden interaction for MSI-high WRN H32, and subtype interaction for EGFR amplification H24. These rejections mean non-support of the registered hypotheses, not proof of exact equality or biological homogeneity.\n\nUnresolved uncertainty. PTEN loss is now a validated dataset-internal STAT3-dependency association, but whether the association survives within Avana-library screens, TP53-wildtype lines, adenocarcinoma subtype, and high-screen-quality lines remains untested through H46-H49. More importantly, the new interactions with TP53 co-loss, KRAS co-mutation, and lineage subtype will test whether the PTEN effect is conditional on co-alterations or confounded by cell-line composition. For the earlier accepted effects, some effect-modification tests remain active, including Avana effect modification for SMARCA4 H22 and screen-quality modification for WRN H35. Broad mechanistic probes remain pending, including MYC amplification with POLR2A or KIF18A dependency H17-H18 and KRAS mutation with RIT1 dependency H19. EGFR-related probes H20 and H43 and the POLR2A RB1 probe H31 are deferred because the EGFR amplification pathway finding failed and because the newly validated PTEN effect deserves higher priority. H29 remains technically unresolved as invalid due to an empty cell; H32 is the better-specified refinement but did not support mutation-burden modification of the MSI-high WRN effect.\n\nNext analysis. The most informative next step is to analyze the PTEN-STAT3 robustness and interaction queue: H46 Avana subset, H47 TP53-wildtype subset, H48 adenocarcinoma subset, H49 screen-quality interaction, and the newly registered H50 TP53-loss interaction, H51 KRAS-mutation interaction, and H52 lineage-subtype interaction. If these remain positive and not strongly modified, PTEN loss would be a robust dataset-internal marker of stronger STAT3 dependency across key assay and genetic subgroups. If modified, the association should be reported as conditional. In parallel, analyze H22 and H35 for possible effect modification of the accepted SMARCA4-SMARCA2 and MSI-high WRN associations, and keep the broad MYC and KRAS-RIT1 probes active. All conclusions remain descriptive associations within the supplied lung-cell-line dataset and may reflect unadjusted confounding by co-mutations, lineage subtype, ploidy, mutation burden, screen quality, CRISPR library, batch, or omics completeness; they do not establish causality or generalizability.",
